[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`fprintf(stderr, "%s requires 4 inputs\n", argv[0])`), so the two binaries can
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m only be compared if they are launched with the *same* `argv[0]`. The test
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m harness does this with `std::os::unix::process::CommandExt::arg0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 1. The sign of a NaN was lost narrowing `double` → `float`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ driver -nan 1 nan
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C   : -nan -nan nan
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: -nan  nan nan      # second component wrong
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main.c` assigns an `atof` result (a `double`) into a `vec3_t` element (a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `float`). The Rust code did this with `atof(...) as f32`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust leaves the resulting NaN bit pattern unspecified for a float-to-float
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `as` cast, and LLVM's constant folder canonicalises it to a *positive* quiet
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m NaN:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | f64 input          | C `(float)` | Rust `as f32` (folded) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |--------------------|-------------|------------------------|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `fff8000000000000` | `ffc00000`  | `7fc00000`             |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m At runtime the two agree, because both end up in `cvtsd2ss`, which preserves the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m sign. The divergence only appeared because LLVM can see through the inlined
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `atof` on the `"nan"` branch — its return value is the compile-time constant
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `-f64::NAN` — and folds the `fptrunc` itself. That lost sign then reaches
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `printf("%f")`, which does print the sign of a NaN.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix** — `src/clib.rs`: `narrow_to_f32`, an explicit narrowing that keeps the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m sign bit and the top 22 significand bits, exactly as `cvtsd2ss` does. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m matching `widen_to_f64` covers the `float` → `double` promotion the variadic
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `printf` argument undergoes (`cvtss2sd`), which has the same hazard.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 2. NaN *selection* differed because LLVM reorders commutative float operands
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom** (still failing after fix 1)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ driver -nan nan 1
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C   : -nan nan -nan
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust:  nan nan  nan
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m On x86, `mulss`/`addss`/`subss` do not return a canonical NaN — they return the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **destination** operand if it is a NaN, otherwise the source operand. Which NaN
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (and therefore which *sign*) comes out is decided purely by operand order.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `gcc -O0` emits the operand order written in the source. LLVM does not have to:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `fadd`/`fmul` are commutative, and LLVM canonicalises their operand order, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `DotProduct`'s `(v0*v0) + (v1*v1)` could come out with the operands swapped.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `v[0] *= k; v[1] *= k; v[2] *= k` vectorises into one `mulps` with the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   broadcast `k` as the *destination*, which makes every lane return `k`'s NaN
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   rather than each `v[i]`'s. That is exactly the `nan nan nan` seen above.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix** — new `src/fpu.rs` with `fmul`/`fadd`/`fsub` that make the destination
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m operand explicit and check for NaN operands before doing the arithmetic, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m optimiser can no longer change which NaN is selected. `q_math.rs` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `q_shared.rs` now use them and preserve C's exact grouping:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `DotProduct` → `fadd(fadd(fmul(x0,y0), fmul(x1,y1)), fmul(x2,y2))`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`+` associates to the left)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `x2 * y * y` → `fmul(fmul(x2, y), y)`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `v[i] *= ilength` → `fmul(v[i], ilength)`, with `v[i]` as the destination
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 3. Invalid operations produce a *negative* NaN on x86
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom** (same investigation as #2)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ driver inf 0 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C: -inf -nan -nan
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `inf * 0`, `inf + -inf` and `inf - inf` are invalid operations. x86 returns the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m "QNaN floating-point indefinite", whose sign bit is **set** (`0xFFC00000`), so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `printf("%f")` renders it `-nan`. LLVM's constant folder produces a *positive*
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m NaN for these instead, so any folded occurrence would have printed `nan`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is reachable from ordinary input: `driver inf 0 0` makes `ilength` `-inf`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and `0 * -inf` is then an invalid operation.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix** — `src/fpu.rs` returns `f32::from_bits(0xFFC00000)` explicitly for these
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m three cases rather than relying on the hardware result surviving optimisation.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 4. Belt-and-braces: the parsed inputs are kept opaque
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m In the C program `Inputs` is filled by an opaque libc call on runtime `argv`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m data, so no part of the subsequent float arithmetic can be constant folded. In
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust, `atof` is inlined and LLVM *can* fold that arithmetic for the constant
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m branches (`"nan"`, `"inf"`, …) — which is how bugs 1–3 became observable in the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m first place.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main.rs` therefore passes the filled array through `std::hint::black_box`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m before `vector_normalize_fast`, restoring the C compilation model. Fixes 1–3 are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the real corrections; this makes them robust to future inlining decisions rather
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m than depending on them.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Not a defect, but worth recording
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`translation/src/cstd.rs` was dead code.** It was never declared as a module
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`main.rs` uses `clib.rs`), so it was never compiled. It was a near-duplicate
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   of `clib.rs` carrying the same `as f32` bug, so it has been deleted rather than
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   left as a second, silently-wrong copy of the parser.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`Q_rsqrt` is an approximation, and the error is visible.** `driver 1 2 3`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   prints `0.267214 0.534428 0.801642`, not the exactly normalized
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `0.267261 0.534522 0.801783`. The single Newton iteration is faithfully
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   reproduced; the output is meant to be "wrong" in this way.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`argc == 0` is not reachable as a distinct case.** Linux (5.18+) inserts an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   empty `argv[0]` when `execve` is handed an empty `argv`, so both programs see
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `argc == 1` and print `" requires 4 inputs"`. Verified with a helper that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `execv`s with an empty `argv`; C and Rust agreed, as they did for `argv[0]`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   values that were empty, contained spaces, or were not valid UTF-8.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **No locale sensitivity.** `main.c` never calls `setlocale`, so the program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   stays in the `"C"` locale and `atof` always uses `.` as the decimal point.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Confirmed identical under `LC_ALL=C`, `en_US.UTF-8`, `de_DE.UTF-8` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `fr_FR.UTF-8`; `1,5` parses as `1` in both.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`atof` NaN payloads do not matter.** `strtod` accepts `nan(n-char-seq)`, but
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `%f` only exposes a NaN's sign, so the payload is unobservable. Only the sign
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   is reproduced.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
